text:
"To the Moon" is a simple story based and enigmatic RPG that will:
- Surprise you;
- Keep you glued to the monitor screen just to see and understand how the plot develops;
- Break your heart and leave you on the corner of the room crying while shiverring.
Highly recommended as the product is innovative in its mini-games and way of proceeding, not to mention all the awesome puns and humour in the dialogues combined with strong sarcasm and irony of the two main characters, which will amuse you for sure.
Perhaps "To the Moon" might not be very long, as a matter of fact you should be able to complete the game in a maximum of 4 hours if you are going to check carefully every single dialogue and click on all the things possible and imaginable to find more information, but it is pretty much understandable that this game made success for a reason, being the amazing experience this emotional story can actually provide you, making every cent you spent on the product acceptable, as myself can assure you that you are not going to forget it any time sooner either.

- The unmodded and true version (besides Game Dev Story)
+ Interesting and mostly unexplored concept for a videogame ;
+ Whether it's an existing videogame or your very own original idea, you can go ahead and try to recreate it - from the 80's text-based dungeon crawlers to the modern era MMOs, expansions and all ;
+ The outcome of what you do seems reasonable and justifiable at all times, which gives the game some credibility ;
+ Managing the economy and team members in this game is approachable and ultimately satisfying ;
+ The characters' progression is visible in the way their work improves ;
+ Some events, namely console releases, occur in chronological order with a good degree of accuracy when set side by side with real life, something I found nostalgic and entertaining ;
+ Funny without even trying ;
- You should have access to some concepts way before the time they're provided to you. The game seems to stall you to give you the feeling of paced progression (ie. you'll probably unlock the "skill trees" engine feature only by the time the PS3 has already been released) ;
- Lacks depth considerably ;
- Some of the game's features are pretty barebones and/or don't feel like working as one would've intended (ie. creating consoles) ;
- Once you've learned the basics and a certain routine settles, the game becomes dull ;
- A bug every now and then ;
