is the product of the work of 2 men, brothers Patrick and Daniel Klug, and a game admittedly inspired by Kairosoft's Game Dev Story (2010) for Android. The premise is simple: you start from the very bottom, developing games from your garage and trying to climb your way up to the top of the video game industry. The amount of variables the game (eventually) provides you to make video games with shows that a lot of thought has been put into this game. This is, in fact, the game's biggest strength. You can muster practically any combination for a video game by tinkering with a bunch of things (topics, genres, target audience, development focus, etc) and have a glimpse at how it would fare in the real world at any given time between the early 80's to well into the 2010's, observing the technology and consoles available at the time. The game will be prepared to rate whatever you release, and it does just that very nicely so, in my opinion. I've put together some games with the sole purpose of emulating their real world counterparts with surgical precision and the results have been pretty believable, as far as ratings, sales, and resources serve me. Overall, I found this system to work well. Game Dev Tycoon is a management game above anything else, really. This is to say that coming in you shouldn't expect to be presented a simulation game. At its core, Game Dev Tycoon succeeds at allowing the player to manage a game developing company. While it intends to simulate being an up-and-coming developer, I don't find that it puts enough effort in trying to feel
like that.
Being a game done by 2 people, I ache to say that the depth is just not there. Being unable to choose a pricing for your games, being unable to see what engine you used on past games and what each engine contains, not having the slightest representation of what your competition is doing, reading the same generic reviews over and over, lacking any sort of resemblance to the reality of what is to present a game at a convention (E3 like), lacking a coherent marketing system for the games, having mindless stat holding bots for employees, giving the player a laughable amount of detail to base the creation of a console upon, among an unfortunate deal of other things, lets this game down bigtime. Between a shallow presentation, features that seemingly didn't get nearly enough attention from the devs and an almost cyclical, slide-dragging spectacle, you'll find a game that, while enjoyable, wears out fast.
Flawed at its hinges,
wasn't destined to be game of the year. I'll take this moment to remember that the year is also 2013, although what Game Dev Tycoon managed to provide the players in 2013 doesn't really differ from what it can provide in 2017, given how
no other game about making video games has even been released since. This game explores a concept that seems hard to do wrong with and manages to create something that feels at least as enjoyable and fun as anyone who finds the concept appealing would expect it to be. Game Dev Tycoon is accessible yet demanding, shallow yet addictive, lighthearted fun.
If the idea of creating your own video games within a game sounds appealing, there's definitely something to draw from this game. I recommend you buy it on sale, can't go wrong for a few bucks. Here's hoping Greenheart Studios pulls through with a
, or someone else does it for them.
6.5/10

